Task 12, the mentors page, passed review with nothing to fix and is done (`3454100`). Four small cosmetic points are parked for the final review.

Task 13, the Docker setup, is now with an implementer, along with three earlier decisions:
- the database, mail viewer and app are only reachable from your own machine;
- the app container stops cleanly when you shut it down;
- the test command rebuilds its image each run, so it never tests old code.

Once Task 13 is in, I'll update one line in the spec so its test command matches, then start Task 14, the README.

Task 13 is approved. The reviewer inspected the live images: no devDependency, test import, PDF or secret ends up in the runtime. Three cheap hardening minors are parked for the final review's fix wave:

- run the runtime as `USER node`;
- widen the ignore pattern to `**/*.pdf`;
- send the healthcheck's output to `/dev/null`.

Marking Task 13 complete and preparing Task 14. First, the `ponytail:` notes the README must list:

Task 13, the Docker setup, passed review and is done (`f5f2314`). The reviewer looked inside the built images: no dev-only packages, tests that the app loads, the assignment PDF or secrets end up in what runs. Three small hardening tweaks are saved for the final round of fixes:
- run the app as a non-root user;
- tighten the rule that keeps PDFs out of the image;
- stop the health check from logging the full slot list every 2 seconds.

Task 14, the last task, is now running on Opus, since this README is the first thing reviewers read. The implementer has a notes file listing every place the build goes beyond the spec, so the README can state each one plainly. Examples are the third test time zone, the date shown when parent and mentor are on different days, and the darker focus ring. After writing it, the implementer does the final check:
- rebuild everything from scratch;
- run all tests in Docker;
- click through the booking, confirmation and mentors pages with the real clock and the demo clock;
- leave the app running on the real clock.

After that comes one final review of all the work. I've gathered its inputs: 19 smaller issues deferred during the per-task reviews.

